Unstructured Data and Knowledge Domain Prioritisation for AI Use Cases

1. Purpose

This page provides an initial prioritisation of:
	•	unstructured and semi-structured data types to be addressed by the workstream
	•	knowledge domains to be prioritised
	•	the method used to derive this view from current AI use case descriptions and architecture discussions

This is intended to support immediate programme decisions and provide a first-cut view for refinement as use case discovery progresses.

2. Context

The bank is building a trusted, AI-ready data foundation to accelerate decision-making, enable responsible AI solutions, and drive measurable business outcomes.

As part of this, the Unstructured Data for AI workstream is focusing on establishing a strategic approach for how unstructured and semi-structured content is:
	•	identified
	•	prioritised
	•	ingested
	•	processed
	•	governed
	•	made available for AI use cases

At this stage, a full source-level inventory is not yet available for every use case. Therefore, the initial prioritisation is being derived from:
	•	current AI use case descriptions
	•	architecture discussions with use case leads
	•	known reference implementations where more detail already exists

3. Approach Used

The initial prioritisation is being developed at two levels:

Level 1 — Data type / modality by use case

Where detailed source information is not yet available, the required data is represented by type, for example:
	•	documents
	•	emails
	•	audio
	•	chat
	•	images
	•	transcripts
	•	semi-structured records

Level 2 — Named source examples

Where architecture understanding is more mature, the prioritisation can be extended to specific named sources or platforms.

For example:
	•	policy and procedure document repositories
	•	contact centre call platforms
	•	knowledge management systems
	•	chat platforms

This allows the workstream to respond quickly while still providing a path toward a more detailed source inventory.

4. Prioritisation Principles

The following principles have been used to determine priority:
	1.	Relevance to active AI use cases
Priority is given to data types and domains already required by in-flight or approved use cases.
	2.	Reuse potential across multiple use cases
Priority is given to data that supports more than one AI use case or pattern.
	3.	Strategic importance to enterprise AI
Priority is given to data types that are foundational for retrieval, grounding, search, knowledge access, and automation.
	4.	Standardisation opportunity
Priority is given to areas where delivery is currently fragmented and would benefit from shared services and controls.
	5.	Feasibility for Q2 progression
Priority is given to areas where enough business and architecture input exists to shape initial standards and target architecture.

