Opening

* This is the latest A1 enterprise reference architecture for unstructured data processing.
* It provides a logical, platform-agnostic view of how heterogeneous unstructured and semi-structured data is onboarded, processed, governed, published, and consumed.
* The architecture is designed to support both runtime AI use cases and enterprise data product exposure.

How to read the diagram

* Read it left to right as the end-to-end lifecycle.
* The top shows the main architecture flow.
* The bottom shows the cross-cutting control plane that applies across all layers.
* A key addition in this version is the Fabric / Data Product Access Layer.

Source domains

* The architecture starts with a broad set of source domains, including internal knowledge, workflow and case systems, collaboration content, media and interaction sources, external content, repositories, and structured data augmentation.
* This makes it clear the platform is designed for heterogeneous sources, not only documents.

Ingestion and onboarding

* This is the controlled front door of the platform.
* It includes source registration, connectors, landing and validation, and policy-aware routing.
* Policy-aware routing is a major design choice, because not all content should follow the same path.

Preparation and normalisation

* This layer converts raw content into a canonical and governed content form.
* It includes format detection, extraction, normalisation, metadata enrichment and classification, and redaction / sensitive data controls.
* This is a key control point before content is indexed, embedded, or exposed to AI services.

Orchestration and semantic processing

* The default model is the deterministic workflow engine.
* Stateful agents are included as a specialised pattern for long-running or multi-step workflows, not as the default for everything.
* This layer also covers chunking, embedding generation, knowledge enrichment, graph build, and quality assurance.

Governed AI integration

* This layer shows how AI runtimes interact with shared AI services in a governed way.
* It includes MCP Client, MCP Gateway, Shared AI Platform Services, and AI Observability and FinOps.
* MCP is positioned as a governed runtime interaction pattern, not as the primary mechanism for all enterprise integration.

Persistence services

* Persistence is separated by purpose.
* This includes raw and processed content, metadata and lineage, analytical outputs, vector, graph, and session state / cache.
* The architecture does not assume one storage model is enough for everything.

Fabric / Data Product Access Layer

* This is one of the key changes in the latest version.
* It shows that outputs from the unstructured platform can be published as governed enterprise data products through Fabric.
* This includes:
    * structured analytical products
    * knowledge graph products
    * curated unstructured products
* This aligns unstructured outputs with the existing enterprise data product access model already used for structured data.

Retrieval and consumption

* This layer supports direct runtime retrieval for search, RAG, assistants, and agentic experiences.
* It also includes retrieval-time governance, which applies entitlement, policy, provenance, and ranking controls.
* Enterprise apps and analytics can consume through this layer as well.

Dual access model

* A key architectural point is that we now support two complementary access patterns:
    1. Published data product access through Fabric
    2. Direct runtime retrieval for latency-sensitive AI use cases
* These are complementary, not competing models.

Cross-cutting controls

* Across all layers, the architecture applies:
    * identity and access
    * policy and DLP
    * lineage and provenance
    * audit and observability
    * encryption and keys
    * legal and trust
    * sovereignty and residency
    * runtime entitlement
    * responsible AI
    * infrastructure and change management
* Governance is built into the architecture, not added later.

Key closing point

* This version establishes the first enterprise architecture baseline for how unstructured data can be processed, governed, published, and consumed across AI use cases.
* It gives us a shared model to align current and future use cases and a foundation for further refinement.

Short closing line

* The main change in this version is the addition of Fabric as the enterprise data product access layer, while retaining direct governed runtime retrieval for interactive AI use cases.


